.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt SORT 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm sort
.Nd sort lines of text (sbase)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl Cbcdfimnru
.Op Fl o Ar outfile
.Op Fl t Ar delim
.Op Fl k Ar def ...
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is sbase 0.1's
.Nm sort ,
built unpatched for ABSD with the C kit; see
.Xr sbase 7 .
It sorts the lines of the named files, or of standard input, by whole
line or by the keys
.Fl k
and
.Fl t
define, and writes them to standard output or, with
.Fl o ,
to
.Ar outfile .
.Fl c
and
.Fl C
check that the input is already sorted.
.Pp
In this pinned sbase version, each
.Fl k
key inherits only the ordering flags that precede it.
Put global flags such as
.Fl n
before every key they should affect, or put the flag in the key definition.
For example,
.Ql sort -n -t , -k 2,2 FILE
and
.Ql sort -t , -k 2,2n FILE
both sort the second comma-separated field numerically.
.Ql sort -t , -k 2,2 -n FILE
instead compares that key lexically and can still return status 0.
This option-order behavior comes from unmodified sbase 0.1.
.Sh UNSUPPORTED ON ABSD
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It input size
in memory, bounded by the 32 MiB heap
.Pq Pa docs/C-INTERFACE.md ;
what happens at exhaustion is the guest qualification's to show.
.It Fl o Ar outfile
writes only where the session may write
.Pq Pa /home/operator , Pa /tmp/session-N .
.It ordering
C locale only (R14): bytes compare as unsigned characters.
.El
.Pp
Behaviour on ABSD is predicted until the guest qualification
.Pq Xr sbase 7 .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on success; with
.Fl c
or
.Fl C ,
1 when the input is not sorted; 2 on an error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
absd$ sort -n -k 2 /home/operator/sizes.txt
absd$ sort -t , -k 2,2n /home/operator/stock.csv
absd$ cat /etc/words.txt | sort | uniq -c
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr intro 1 ,
.Xr sbase 7
